+/* =========================================================================================
+ * CD32X SCREEN SPLIT - how the two SH2s share the drawing  (SPLIT-1 + SPLIT-2, CD32X only)
+ * -----------------------------------------------------------------------------------------
+ * Original OpenLara 32X: both SH2s walk the ordering table (OT) together and take every
+ *   other polygon (index & 1).  To keep the painter's order (far before near) correct where
+ *   their polygons overlap, they stop after EVERY depth layer and wait for each other
+ *   (COMM2 / COMM6 handshake).
+ *   Profiler on hardware (7-9 fps): the master lost 16-30 ms per frame in those waits.
+ *
+ * SPLIT-1: the master draws screen rows [0, split), the slave rows [split, 224).
+ *   Each SH2 walks the WHOLE OT far -> near and draws only what falls in its own rows, so the
+ *   painter's order is right inside each half and the SH2s never wait for each other while
+ *   drawing.  A polygon crossing the split line is clipped to the half (drawPolyY); sprites
+ *   clip in rasterizeSprite_c (rows passed in VertexLink.padding); lines and fills are cut in
+ *   flush_ot.  The master tells the slave where the line is through COMM2 (flush_c).
+ *   Result on hardware: exploring 7-9 fps -> 9-14 fps; the end-of-frame wait S is 0-5 ms.
+ *
+ * SPLIT-2: the line stays still.  SPLIT-1 moved it 4 rows EVERY frame to balance the load.
+ *   A polygon that crosses the line is cut into two pieces, and a cut piece places its texels
+ *   very slightly differently (the cut point is rounded to a whole pixel and the texture
+ *   stepping restarts there).  With the line moving every frame, a still picture flashed:
+ *   pixels near the middle of the screen changed for one frame and changed back.  Now the line
+ *   moves (4 rows) only when one SH2 has been clearly late for 8 frames in a row (see flush_c),
+ *   so a still picture is identical every frame.  Confirmed on hardware: no more flashing.
+ *
+ * Why the line is usually NOT in the middle: the 32X SH2s are master and slave on one shared
+ *   bus.  The master owns the bus; the slave must ask for it for every memory access, so while
+ *   both draw (framebuffer writes, texture reads) the slave stalls and gets less done.  The
+ *   balancing gives the slave fewer rows when it falls behind.  Work that runs mostly from the
+ *   SH2's own cache suits the slave better than drawing does.
+ *
+ * Switch off: build with -DCD32X_NO_SPLIT (back to the original layer-by-layer sharing).
+ * ========================================================================================= */
